1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,613
This is STAT 331, video 3. The topic is the simple linear regression model.

2
00:00:07,063 --> 00:00:13,527
This video prepares you for four question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:13,977 --> 00:00:20,591
Type A: compute sample summaries. These are the mean, the variance, the covariance and the correlation.

4
00:00:21,041 --> 00:00:30,342
Type B: find the distribution of y i under the model, for example to simulate data, as in Assignment 1, question 4.

5
00:00:30,792 --> 00:00:37,192
Type C: interpret beta zero and beta one, and their estimates, in the units of the problem.

6
00:00:37,642 --> 00:00:44,575
Type D: state the four model assumptions for a given set-up, as in Tutorial 2, question 2.

7
00:00:45,025 --> 00:00:59,831
Section 1 reviews the tools of Lecture 2. The mean, or expectation, of a random variable Y is the integral of y times f of y. It is the centre of the density. Here E of Y is 1.

8
00:01:00,281 --> 00:01:11,545
Expectation is linear. The expectation of a sum of a i Y i plus b i is the sum of a i times E of Y i, plus the sum of the b i.

9
00:01:11,995 --> 00:01:21,232
Use the rule on 2 Y plus 1. Here a is 2 and b is 1. So E of 2 Y plus 1 is 2 times E of Y, plus 1.

10
00:01:21,682 --> 00:01:32,775
E of Y is 1. So the mean is 2 times 1 plus 1, which is 3. The dashed curve is the density of 2 Y plus 1. Its centre is at 3.

11
00:01:33,225 --> 00:01:46,281
For observed data y 1 to y n, the sample mean y bar is the sum of the y i, divided by n. The dots are the brain weights of the first five people of the brainhead data.

12
00:01:46,731 --> 00:01:59,254
The five weights add to 7034. 7034 over 5 is 1406.8 grams. It is the centre of the five dots.

13
00:01:59,704 --> 00:02:21,848
The variance of Y is the expected squared distance from the mean. Write mu for E of Y. Slide 5 gives a second form. Pause the video and try to show it. On the right, an example: Y is 0 or 2, each with probability one half. Mu is 1. Each distance is 1, so line 1 gives a variance of 1.

14
00:02:22,298 --> 00:02:29,850
Line 2: expand the square. Y minus mu, squared, is Y squared minus 2 mu Y plus mu squared.

15
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:43,676
Line 3: use linearity. Mu is a constant, so it comes out of the expectation. In the example, E of Y squared is one half times 0, plus one half times 4, which is 2.

16
00:02:44,126 --> 00:02:49,630
Line 4: E of Y is mu. So 2 mu times E of Y is 2 mu squared.

17
00:02:50,080 --> 00:03:03,499
Line 5: collect the mu squared terms. The variance is E of Y squared minus E of Y, squared. In the example, 2 minus 1 squared is 1. This is the same as line 1.

18
00:03:03,949 --> 00:03:08,983
So the variance has two forms. Both forms appear in later derivations.

19
00:03:09,433 --> 00:03:25,028
Slide 5 lists two properties. The first: the variance of a Y plus b is a squared times the variance of Y. Pause the video and try to show it. Line 1 is the definition of the variance of a Y plus b.

20
00:03:25,478 --> 00:03:30,107
Line 2: by linearity, E of a Y plus b is a mu plus b.

21
00:03:30,557 --> 00:03:43,720
Line 3: put line 2 into line 1. The b cancels. On the right, Y plus 3 has the same spread as Y. Adding a constant moves the density, but the spread stays the same.

22
00:03:44,170 --> 00:03:55,242
Line 4: factor out a from the bracket. On the right, 2 Y plus 6 has a standard deviation of 1. That is 2 times the standard deviation of Y.

23
00:03:55,692 --> 00:04:09,879
Line 5: a squared is a constant, so it comes out of the expectation. The rest is the variance of Y. So the standard deviation doubles, and the variance is 2 squared, which is 4, times larger.

24
00:04:10,329 --> 00:04:19,395
The second property: for independent X and Y, the variance of X plus Y is the variance of X plus the variance of Y.

25
00:04:19,845 --> 00:04:34,053
The covariance of X and Y is the expected product of the two deviations from the means. The plot shows the first five people of the brainhead data. The purple dashed lines are x bar and y bar.

26
00:04:34,503 --> 00:04:43,677
Each point makes a rectangle with the mean point. Its signed area is x i minus x bar, times y i minus y bar.

27
00:04:44,127 --> 00:04:56,884
Upper right or lower left gives a positive product, in blue. Upper left or lower right gives a negative product, in red. Person 3 is lower right, so the product is negative.

28
00:04:57,334 --> 00:05:02,689
The sample covariance adds the signed areas and divides by n minus 1.

29
00:05:03,139 --> 00:05:08,323
Three properties. The covariance of X with itself is the variance of X.

30
00:05:08,773 --> 00:05:15,173
Constants a and b multiply the covariance. The added constants c and d have no effect.

31
00:05:15,623 --> 00:05:20,060
The covariance of two sums expands into all pairs of terms.

32
00:05:20,510 --> 00:05:35,635
The correlation coefficient rho divides the covariance by the two standard deviations. It measures the strength of a linear relationship. The plot on the right shows all 236 people of the brainhead data.

33
00:05:36,085 --> 00:05:45,835
The sample correlation r puts the sample versions into this formula. Each of the three sums has a factor 1 over n minus 1.

34
00:05:46,285 --> 00:05:57,805
Give the marked sums their names. The sum of the products of the deviations is S x y. The sums of the squared deviations are S y y and S x x.

35
00:05:58,255 --> 00:06:11,801
The two square roots each hold 1 over n minus 1. The root of a times the root of a is a. So the bottom is 1 over n minus 1 times the root of S x x times S y y.

36
00:06:12,251 --> 00:06:25,947
Cancel 1 over n minus 1 on the top and on the bottom. r equals S x y over the square root of S x x times S y y. Question type A uses this in step 4.

37
00:06:26,397 --> 00:06:35,912
For all 236 people of the brainhead data, r is 0.79. The points show a clear upward linear trend.

38
00:06:36,362 --> 00:06:48,501
Two sets of our own simulated data. With no linear trend, r is near 0. With a downward trend, r is negative. r is always between minus 1 and 1.

39
00:06:48,951 --> 00:06:59,617
Question type A asks for sample summaries: the mean, the variance, the covariance or the correlation. The data come as a table or as computer output.

40
00:07:00,067 --> 00:07:06,083
Method card A has five steps. Step 1: find the means, x bar and y bar.

41
00:07:06,533 --> 00:07:12,720
Step 2: find the deviations from the means. Each column of deviations adds to 0.

42
00:07:13,170 --> 00:07:20,914
Step 3: find the three sums, S x x, S y y and S x y. With computer output, read them.

43
00:07:21,364 --> 00:07:38,601
Step 4: divide. The sample variance is S y y over n minus 1. The sample covariance is S x y over n minus 1. The correlation r is S x y over the root of S x x times S y y.

44
00:07:39,051 --> 00:07:48,033
Step 5: check. A variance is never negative. r has the sign of S x y. And r is between minus 1 and 1.

45
00:07:48,483 --> 00:08:04,781
A type A problem with the slide data. Pause the video and try it. The table has the head size x and the brain weight y of the first five people. Find y bar, the sample variance of y, the sample covariance, and r.

46
00:08:05,231 --> 00:08:22,938
Step 1 of card A: the means. The x values add to 20465, so x bar is 4093. The y values add to 7034, so y bar is 1406.8 grams.

47
00:08:23,388 --> 00:08:42,268
Step 2: the deviations. In row 1, 4512 minus 4093 is plus 419, and 1530 minus 1406.8 is plus 123.2.

48
00:08:42,718 --> 00:08:48,819
Step 5 is a check, and we can do it now. Each deviation column adds to 0.

49
00:08:49,269 --> 00:09:06,059
Step 3: square the y deviations. In row 1, 123.2 squared is 15178.24. The sum is S y y, which is 81526.8.

50
00:09:06,509 --> 00:09:22,573
Square the x deviations. In row 1, 419 squared is 175561. The sum is S x x, which is 436722.

51
00:09:23,023 --> 00:09:44,100
Multiply the two deviations in each row. In row 1, the product is plus 51620.8. The sum is S x y, which is 133363. On the right, it is the total signed area of the rectangles. Person 1 is marked.

52
00:09:44,550 --> 00:10:04,881
Step 4: divide. The sample variance is S y y over n minus 1. 81526.8 over 4 is 20381.7 grams squared. The standard deviation is the root, 142.8 grams.

53
00:10:05,331 --> 00:10:17,747
The sample covariance is S x y over 4. 133363 over 4 is 33340.75.

54
00:10:18,197 --> 00:10:44,863
r is S x y over the root of S x x times S y y. The root of 436722 times 81526.8 is 188692. 133363 over 188692 is 0.707.

55
00:10:45,313 --> 00:10:57,793
Step 5: check. The variance is positive. The covariance and r are both positive, like S x y. And 0.707 is between minus 1 and 1.

56
00:10:58,243 --> 00:11:14,243
A second type A problem, now from computer output. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 1, question 2, gives R output for 100 aircraft. Find the sample correlation between flight hours and maintenance cost.

57
00:11:14,693 --> 00:11:26,107
R did steps 1 to 3 of card A for us. Step 1: the means are 148.8 hours and 1822 thousand dollars.

58
00:11:26,557 --> 00:11:35,901
Step 3: read the sums. The sum of the squared x deviations is S x x, 731093.

59
00:11:36,351 --> 00:11:45,524
The sum of the squared y deviations is S y y, 47854282.

60
00:11:45,974 --> 00:11:53,803
The sum of the products of the deviations is S x y, 4686071.

61
00:11:54,253 --> 00:11:59,800
Step 4: r is S x y over the root of S x x times S y y.

62
00:12:00,250 --> 00:12:25,594
The root of 731093 times 47854282 is 5914891. 4686071 over 5914891 is 0.792.

63
00:12:26,044 --> 00:12:38,844
Step 5: check. 0.792 is between minus 1 and 1. It is positive, like S x y, and like the slope estimate 6.410 in the output.

64
00:12:39,294 --> 00:12:54,654
Section 2 is the model. First the set-up of slide 8. For observation i, y i is the outcome, and x i is the covariate. The index i goes from 1 to n. Each point on the plot is one person.

65
00:12:55,104 --> 00:13:06,517
For example, person 1 has x 1 equal to 4512 cubic centimetres and y 1 equal to 1530 grams.

66
00:13:06,967 --> 00:13:20,493
There are two possible goals. First, describe how y changes with x. Second, predict y for a given x. For example, what brain weight do we expect for a head size of 4000?

67
00:13:20,943 --> 00:13:34,447
The data example of the course is the brainhead data from 1905. It has 236 people. x is head size in cubic centimetres. y is brain weight in grams.

68
00:13:34,897 --> 00:13:39,142
The table shows the first six people, as slide 10 prints them.

69
00:13:39,592 --> 00:13:48,445
On the plot, these six people are the dark blue points with their numbers. Person 1 is marked. The light points are the other people.

70
00:13:48,895 --> 00:13:57,471
Before we fit a model, we look at the data. This is exploratory data analysis. The first histogram shows head size.

71
00:13:57,921 --> 00:14:06,135
Head size goes from 2773 to 4747 cubic centimetres.

72
00:14:06,585 --> 00:14:08,931
The second histogram shows brain weight.

73
00:14:09,381 --> 00:14:15,205
Brain weight goes from 1012 to 1635 grams.

74
00:14:15,655 --> 00:14:23,143
Both histograms have one peak, near the middle, and they are roughly symmetric. No value is far from the others.

75
00:14:23,593 --> 00:14:31,209
The scatter plot shows the two variables together. Each point is one person: head size across, brain weight up.

76
00:14:31,659 --> 00:14:38,977
The purple dashed lines are x bar and y bar. Most points are in the upper right and the lower left parts.

77
00:14:39,427 --> 00:14:53,144
So people with a larger head size tend to have a larger brain weight. The trend is close to a straight line, and the correlation is 0.79. A linear model is a reasonable start.

78
00:14:53,594 --> 00:15:10,149
Here is the simple linear regression model. y i equals beta zero, plus beta one times x i, plus epsilon i. The gray dashed line is beta zero plus beta one x. It is the true line, and it is unknown.

79
00:15:10,599 --> 00:15:26,428
Each observation is the value on the line, plus an error epsilon i. The orange segments are the errors. Epsilon 1 is above the line, so it is positive. Epsilon 4 is below the line, so it is negative.

80
00:15:26,878 --> 00:15:41,299
The errors are iid normal, with mean 0 and variance sigma squared. The orange curve shows this normal distribution at one x. Most errors are small. Large errors are less likely.

81
00:15:41,749 --> 00:15:51,136
iid means independent and identically distributed. Each error has the same distribution, and no error depends on another.

82
00:15:51,586 --> 00:15:59,906
In practice, we observe only the points. We do not see the true line or the errors. We learn about the line from the points.

83
00:16:00,356 --> 00:16:12,367
The model has different kinds of quantities. Look at how one observation is made. First, x i. We treat x i as fixed. It is a known number, not a random variable.

84
00:16:12,817 --> 00:16:23,889
Then the mean, beta zero plus beta one x i. Beta zero, beta one and sigma squared are parameters. They are fixed numbers, but we do not know them.

85
00:16:24,339 --> 00:16:29,373
Then we add the error epsilon i. It is random, and we cannot observe it.

86
00:16:29,823 --> 00:16:43,306
The result is y i. It is random, because epsilon i is random. And we observe it. So the data are the pairs x i, y i. From the data, we estimate the three parameters.

87
00:16:43,756 --> 00:16:53,633
Question type B asks for the distribution of y i, for a given x. Assignment 1, question 4, needs it to simulate data.

88
00:16:54,083 --> 00:17:08,206
Method card B has four steps. Step 1: the mean. E of y i is beta zero plus beta one x i. Put in the value of x i. On the right, the mean is the point on the line.

89
00:17:08,656 --> 00:17:18,491
Step 2: the variance of y i is sigma squared. It is the same at every x. The orange bar is one standard deviation, sigma.

90
00:17:18,941 --> 00:17:25,362
Step 3: y i is normal, with this mean and this variance, and the y i are independent.

91
00:17:25,812 --> 00:17:40,724
Step 4, if the question asks for a probability: standardize. z is c minus the mean, over sigma. Then the probability that y i is larger than c is 1 minus Phi of z. It is the red area.

92
00:17:41,174 --> 00:17:56,470
Slide 13 gives a second form of the model. Card B derives it. Pause the video and try it. Line 1 is the model. y i is a constant, beta zero plus beta one x i, plus a random error.

93
00:17:56,920 --> 00:18:07,416
Step 1 of card B: the mean. By linearity, E of y i is E of beta zero plus beta one x i, plus E of epsilon i.

94
00:18:07,866 --> 00:18:21,690
Beta zero, beta one and x i are constants, so the first part is beta zero plus beta one x i. E of epsilon i is 0. So the mean of y i is on the line.

95
00:18:22,140 --> 00:18:30,908
Step 2: the variance. y i is epsilon i plus the constant b, which is beta zero plus beta one x i.

96
00:18:31,358 --> 00:18:46,739
The variance of a Y plus b is a squared times the variance of Y. Here a is 1. So the variance of y i is the variance of epsilon i, sigma squared. The spread of y i is the spread of the error.

97
00:18:47,189 --> 00:19:09,547
Step 3: a normal variable plus a constant is normal. The errors are independent, so the y i are independent. So y i is independent normal, with mean beta zero plus beta one x i, and variance sigma squared. On the right, every normal curve has its centre on the line and the same width.

98
00:19:09,997 --> 00:19:23,714
This is the equivalent form. The y i are independent, but they are not identically distributed: each y i has its own mean. Type B uses steps 1 to 3 of this derivation.

99
00:19:24,164 --> 00:19:51,684
A type B problem from Assignment 1, question 4. The true model has ten known x values. Part b uses beta zero equal to 1, beta one equal to 0.25, and sigma equal to 1. Which normal distribution does the R function r norm use for Y 1 and for Y 2? We add one part: the probability that Y 2 is larger than 4.5. Pause the video and try it.

100
00:19:52,134 --> 00:20:02,609
Step 1 of card B: the mean. x 1 is 1. So E of Y 1 is 1 plus 0.25 times 1, which is 1.25.

101
00:20:03,059 --> 00:20:10,760
x 2 is 10. So E of Y 2 is 1 plus 0.25 times 10, which is 3.5.

102
00:20:11,210 --> 00:20:16,245
Step 2: the variance is sigma squared. 1 squared is 1, for every i.

103
00:20:16,695 --> 00:20:29,324
Step 3: Y 1 is normal with mean 1.25 and variance 1. Y 2 is normal with mean 3.5 and variance 1. These are the two r norm calls.

104
00:20:29,774 --> 00:20:40,099
Step 4: standardize. 4.5 minus 3.5, over 1, is 1. So we need the probability that Z is larger than 1.

105
00:20:40,549 --> 00:20:54,373
That is 1 minus Phi of 1. Phi of 1 is 0.8413, so the probability is 0.1587. It is the red area of the curve at x equals 10.

106
00:20:54,823 --> 00:21:06,471
Part c sets beta one to 0. Then every mean is 1, and every Y i is normal with mean 1 and variance 1. The mean of Y does not depend on x.

107
00:21:06,921 --> 00:21:19,636
Part d sets sigma to 0.5. The variance is 0.5 squared, which is 0.25. The means stay the same, and the normal curves are narrower.

108
00:21:20,086 --> 00:21:32,950
Section 3 is the meaning of the two coefficients. Slide 14: the mean of y i, given x i, is beta zero plus beta one x i. This is the regression line.

109
00:21:33,400 --> 00:21:39,373
Put x i equal to 0. The mean is beta zero plus beta one times 0.

110
00:21:39,823 --> 00:21:52,175
Beta one times 0 is 0. So the mean of y at x equal to 0 is beta zero. On the plot, beta zero is the height of the line at x equal to 0.

111
00:21:52,625 --> 00:22:01,777
So beta zero is the intercept. It is the mean outcome for units with x equal to 0. Type C uses this in step 3.

112
00:22:02,227 --> 00:22:11,571
Now the slope, slide 16. Take any value x star. The mean of y at x star is beta zero plus beta one x star.

113
00:22:12,021 --> 00:22:20,064
Go up by one unit, to x star plus 1. The mean is beta zero plus beta one times x star plus 1.

114
00:22:20,514 --> 00:22:22,733
Subtract line 1 from line 2.

115
00:22:23,183 --> 00:22:33,679
Beta zero cancels, and beta one x star cancels. Only beta one stays. On the plot, it is the rise of the line over a run of 1.

116
00:22:34,129 --> 00:22:46,758
So beta one is the change in the mean of y when x increases by one unit. It is the same for every x star, because the line is straight. Type C uses this in step 2.

117
00:22:47,208 --> 00:22:58,877
Lecture 3 gives a more careful wording. Think of all units with x equal to x star. This is one population. Its y values have a normal distribution.

118
00:22:59,327 --> 00:23:04,383
All units with x equal to x star plus 1 make a second population.

119
00:23:04,833 --> 00:23:19,639
Beta one is the difference between the two population means. It compares a population with x to a population with x one unit lower. It does not say that the y of one unit changes if we change its x.

120
00:23:20,089 --> 00:23:27,321
Beta one hat, from the data, is an estimate of this mean difference. Use this wording in exam answers.

121
00:23:27,771 --> 00:23:36,411
Question type C asks you to interpret beta zero and beta one, or their estimates. Method card C has four steps.

122
00:23:36,861 --> 00:23:42,834
Step 1: write the units of x and of y. Every sentence of the answer uses them.

123
00:23:43,284 --> 00:23:58,879
Step 2: beta one is the mean difference in y, comparing a population with x to a population with x one unit lower. For beta one hat, say estimated. The sign says higher or lower. The triangle shows it.

124
00:23:59,329 --> 00:24:07,073
Step 3: beta zero is the mean of y when x is 0. It is the height of the line at x equal to 0.

125
00:24:07,523 --> 00:24:17,571
Step 4: check the range of x in the data. If x equal to 0 is far outside this range, beta zero hat has no practical meaning.

126
00:24:18,021 --> 00:24:35,855
A type C problem. Pause the video and try it. Slide 38 gives the estimates for the brainhead data. Beta zero hat is 335.4323, and beta one hat is 0.2608. Interpret both.

127
00:24:36,305 --> 00:24:43,367
Step 1 of card C: x is head size in cubic centimetres, and y is brain weight in grams.

128
00:24:43,817 --> 00:25:06,985
Step 2: comparing populations with head sizes one cubic centimetre apart, the estimated mean brain weight is 0.26 grams higher in the larger one. One cubic centimetre is small, so we can also say 260.8 grams per 1000 cubic centimetres. The triangle shows this rise.

129
00:25:07,435 --> 00:25:21,856
Step 3: beta zero hat, 335.4 grams, is the estimated mean brain weight at head size 0. We must extend the line far to the left to reach x equal to 0.

130
00:25:22,306 --> 00:25:40,802
Step 4: the head sizes in the data go from 2773 to 4747. Head size 0 is far outside this range. So beta zero hat has no practical meaning here. It only fixes the height of the line.

131
00:25:41,252 --> 00:25:59,407
A type C problem from Tutorial 2, question 2 a. Pause the video and try it. x i is 0 for the first n 1 subjects, group 0, and 1 for the others, group 1. Interpret beta one, and write a null hypothesis for no difference in means.

132
00:25:59,857 --> 00:26:08,667
Step 1 of card C: x has no units here. x equal to 0 means group 0, and x equal to 1 means group 1.

133
00:26:09,117 --> 00:26:15,901
Step 2 needs the two means. Put x equal to 0: the mean of group 0 is beta zero.

134
00:26:16,351 --> 00:26:21,535
Put x equal to 1: the mean of group 1 is beta zero plus beta one.

135
00:26:21,985 --> 00:26:35,404
Subtract line 1 from line 2. Beta one is the mean of group 1 minus the mean of group 0. This is the answer: beta one is the difference in mean response between the two groups.

136
00:26:35,854 --> 00:26:40,035
Step 3: beta zero is the mean response of group 0.

137
00:26:40,485 --> 00:26:48,336
Step 4: x equal to 0 is one of the two groups in the data. So here beta zero has a practical meaning.

138
00:26:48,786 --> 00:27:03,442
No difference in means is the same as beta one equal to 0. So test H 0, beta one equals 0, against H 1, beta one is not 0. Video 8 shows how to do the test.

139
00:27:03,892 --> 00:27:24,137
A type C problem from Tutorial 2, question 1. Pause the video and try it. y is patient satisfaction, and x is the severity of the illness. Part c asks to interpret the estimated association. Part f asks if we can trust a prediction at severity 100.

140
00:27:24,587 --> 00:27:31,883
Step 1 of card C: y is a satisfaction score, and x is a severity index. Both are in points.

141
00:27:32,333 --> 00:27:50,403
Step 2: beta one hat is minus 2.3283. Patients with severity one point higher have an estimated mean satisfaction 2.33 points lower. The triangle shows 10 points more severity: 23.3 points lower.

142
00:27:50,853 --> 00:28:00,197
Step 3: beta zero hat, 179.13, is the estimated mean satisfaction at severity 0.

143
00:28:00,647 --> 00:28:14,129
Step 4: the range of x. The output gives only x bar and S x x. But each squared deviation is one term of S x x, so it is at most 532.24.

144
00:28:14,579 --> 00:28:20,681
Take the root. Each x i is at most 23.07 from 50.48.

145
00:28:21,131 --> 00:28:34,101
So every severity in the data is between 27.41 and 73.55. Severity 0 is outside, so beta zero hat has no practical meaning.

146
00:28:34,551 --> 00:28:52,151
Part f: severity 100 is larger than 73.55, so it is outside the data. The tutorial solution says the largest severity is 62. A prediction at 100 is an extrapolation, so we do not trust it.

147
00:28:52,601 --> 00:28:59,727
One more type C problem, with the estimates of Tutorial 1, question 2. Pause the video and try it.

148
00:29:00,177 --> 00:29:07,878
Step 1 of card C: y is the maintenance cost in thousands of dollars. x is the number of flight hours.

149
00:29:08,328 --> 00:29:23,688
Step 2: beta one hat is 6.410. Aircraft with one more flight hour have an estimated mean cost 6.410 thousand dollars higher. That is 6410 dollars.

150
00:29:24,138 --> 00:29:35,786
For 50 more flight hours, the difference is 50 times 6.410, which is 320.5 thousand dollars. The triangle shows it.

151
00:29:36,236 --> 00:29:45,559
Step 3: beta zero hat, 868.487, is the estimated mean cost at 0 flight hours.

152
00:29:46,009 --> 00:30:00,302
Step 4: the output gives x bar, 148.8 hours, but not the range of x. We cannot tell if x equal to 0 is near the data. So use beta zero hat with care.

153
00:30:00,752 --> 00:30:14,384
Section 4 is the assumptions. Slide 18 lists four. One: linearity. The mean of y is a straight-line function of x. This comes from beta zero plus beta one x i.

154
00:30:14,834 --> 00:30:24,647
Two: independence. The errors are independent. One error gives no information about another error. This comes from the i of iid.

155
00:30:25,097 --> 00:30:34,441
Three: normality. The errors have a normal distribution. So at each x, y has a normal distribution. This comes from the N.

156
00:30:34,891 --> 00:30:43,361
Four: equal variance. The variance sigma squared is the same at every x. The name for this is homoskedasticity.

157
00:30:43,811 --> 00:30:47,800
So each assumption comes from one part of the model equation.

158
00:30:48,250 --> 00:31:00,133
Question type D asks what the model assumptions say for a given set-up. A question often says: with the usual assumptions. Method card D lists them in a fixed order.

159
00:31:00,583 --> 00:31:08,540
Step 1: linearity. The mean of y i is beta zero plus beta one x i. The means lie on a line.

160
00:31:08,990 --> 00:31:14,707
Step 2: independence. The errors are independent, so the y i are independent.

161
00:31:15,157 --> 00:31:20,192
Step 3: normality. Each error is normal, so each y i is normal.

162
00:31:20,642 --> 00:31:30,327
Step 4: equal variance. The variance of each error is sigma squared, the same at every x. The orange bars have the same length.

163
00:31:30,777 --> 00:31:46,649
A type D problem from Tutorial 2, question 2. Pause the video and try it. x is 0 for group 0 and 1 for group 1. The model has the usual assumptions. What do they say about the responses of each group?

164
00:31:47,099 --> 00:31:58,086
Step 1 of card D: linearity. With x equal to 0, the mean is beta zero. With x equal to 1, the mean is beta zero plus beta one.

165
00:31:58,536 --> 00:32:03,955
Step 2: independence. The responses of all n subjects are independent.

166
00:32:04,405 --> 00:32:07,882
Step 3: normality. Each response is normal.

167
00:32:08,332 --> 00:32:17,185
Step 4: equal variance. The variance is sigma squared in both groups. On the right, the two orange bars have the same length.

168
00:32:17,635 --> 00:32:32,718
So group 0 is normal with mean beta zero, group 1 is normal with mean beta zero plus beta one, and both have variance sigma squared. This is the usual two-sample problem with equal variances.

169
00:32:33,168 --> 00:32:48,208
This slide is optional. It is not on the slides. It shows two data sets of our own. In the left plot, the mean of y follows a curve. A straight line misses the pattern. Linearity, step 1 of card D, fails.

170
00:32:48,658 --> 00:32:56,743
In the right plot, the spread of y grows as x grows. The points make a fan shape. Equal variance, step 4, fails.

171
00:32:57,193 --> 00:33:05,193
Later lectures check the assumptions with residual plots. For now, look at the scatter plot before you use the model.

172
00:33:05,643 --> 00:33:20,726
Slide 18 ends with a question: how do we estimate beta zero and beta one from the data? Here is one candidate line, with slope 0.10. The orange segments show how far the points are from it.

173
00:33:21,176 --> 00:33:28,579
Here is a steeper candidate line, with slope 0.45. Again, many points are far from it.

174
00:33:29,029 --> 00:33:36,154
We need a rule that picks one line. Video 4 gives the rule: least squares. It gives the green line.

175
00:33:36,604 --> 00:33:51,196
To recap. Card A computes sample summaries: the means, the deviations, the three sums, then divide, then check. The correlation is S x y over the root of S x x times S y y.

176
00:33:51,646 --> 00:34:04,318
Card B gives the distribution of y i: mean beta zero plus beta one x i, variance sigma squared, normal and independent. For a probability, standardize.

177
00:34:04,768 --> 00:34:18,315
Card C interprets the coefficients: the units, beta one as a mean difference for x one unit higher, beta zero as the mean at x equal to 0, and a check of the range of x.

178
00:34:18,765 --> 00:34:30,733
Card D states the four assumptions: linearity, independence, normality and equal variance. The next video estimates beta zero and beta one by least squares.
